WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.319
For seven days our ship had been tossed at sea.

2
00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.982
The broken masts had gone overboard.

3
00:00:15.983 --> 00:00:21.865
Leaks had sprung everywhere, and the water was rising rapidly as the raging storm grew stronger.

4
00:00:21.866 --> 00:00:26.887
My wife, Elizabeth, gathered our four young sons around her in our cabin.

5
00:00:26.888 --> 00:00:32.080
She was encouraging them with calm and loving words.

6
00:00:32.081 --> 00:00:36.900
This was not the scene we had pictured when we set out from Switzerland to establish a new colony.

7
00:00:36.901 --> 00:00:44.081
Amid the roar of the thundering waves, I suddenly heard a seaman cry, "Land! Land!"

8
00:00:44.082 --> 00:00:49.952
At the same instant, the ship struck something with a jolt that threw everyone to the deck of the cabin.

9
00:00:49.953 --> 00:00:57.747
Above the dreadful sounds of the ship breaking up, I heard the captain shout, "Lower the lifeboats! Abandon ship!"

10
00:00:57.748 --> 00:01:03.074
"Abandon ship!" I exclaimed. The words went like a dagger through my heart.

11
00:01:03.075 --> 00:01:06.879
But seeing my children's terror, I hid my own fear.

12
00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:11.674
"Take courage, my boys!" I cried. "We're all still above water.

13
00:01:11.675 --> 00:01:15.799
There is land nearby. Let's do our best to reach it."

14
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:18.183
With that I went up on deck.

15
00:01:18.184 --> 00:01:22.515
Imagine my horror when I saw all the lifeboats out on the water.

16
00:01:22.516 --> 00:01:24.693
The crew had abandoned us!

17
00:01:24.694 --> 00:01:30.619
But as I looked around in despair, I realized that our situation was not hopeless.

18
00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:36.169
Although the bow of the ship had been destroyed, our cabin remained unbroken in the stern.

19
00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:39.608
I could just make out some land through the rain.

20
00:01:39.609 --> 00:01:42.491
I returned to my family and forced a smile.

21
00:01:42.492 --> 00:01:46.451
"If the wind and waves die down tomorrow, we can go ashore."

22
00:01:46.452 --> 00:01:49.756
"Then we must find food," said my wife.

23
00:01:49.757 --> 00:01:53.458
"We'll need all our strength tomorrow."

24
00:01:53.459 --> 00:02:02.191
After a good supper, the three youngest boys—Ernest, 13; Jack, 11; and Franz, 8—were soon asleep.

25
00:02:02.192 --> 00:02:07.324
But at 15, Fritz was old enough to understand the danger and to stay up with us.

26
00:02:07.325 --> 00:02:10.412
"Father, you and I can swim," he said.

27
00:02:10.413 --> 00:02:14.827
"But don't you think we should make life preservers for Mother and the boys?"

28
00:02:14.828 --> 00:02:17.358
"An excellent idea!" I told Fritz.

29
00:02:17.359 --> 00:02:21.536
"Let's make some right now in case we need to escape the ship during the night."

30
00:02:21.537 --> 00:02:26.254
We searched the ship and soon got hold of enough empty flasks and canisters.

31
00:02:26.255 --> 00:02:35.045
Connecting them together, we made life preservers, which we all quickly put on.

32
00:02:35.046 --> 00:02:42.319
Fritz and his brothers now slept soundly while Elizabeth and I kept watch all

33
00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:45.777
night. The next morning we gathered on the remaining portion of the

34
00:02:45.778 --> 00:02:49.764
deck. "Where are the sailors?" asked my youngest son,

35
00:02:49.765 --> 00:02:53.547
Franz. "Have they taken away all the boats?" asked

36
00:02:53.548 --> 00:02:55.287
Jack. I nodded

37
00:02:55.288 --> 00:03:00.227
gravely. "The sea will soon be calm enough for swimming," Fritz reassured his younger

38
00:03:00.228 --> 00:03:03.711
brothers. "That's fine for you and Father!" exclaimed

39
00:03:03.712 --> 00:03:06.242
Ernest. "But think of Mother and the rest of

40
00:03:06.243 --> 00:03:09.763
us! Why not build a raft so we can all go ashore

41
00:03:09.764 --> 00:03:13.853
together?" "It would be difficult to build a raft that would carry us safely," I

42
00:03:13.854 --> 00:03:18.514
said. "But let's think about that while we explore the ship to see what might be of use to

43
00:03:18.515 --> 00:03:24.571
us." While I checked on the food and water, my wife took Franz to help her feed the animals on

44
00:03:24.572 --> 00:03:27.270
board. Jack went to the captain's

45
00:03:27.271 --> 00:03:37.177
cabin. When he opened the door, out sprang two large but friendly

46
00:03:37.178 --> 00:03:40.099
dogs! Back in our cabin we displayed our

47
00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:44.747
treasures. Fritz brought a couple of guns, powder flasks, and plenty of

48
00:03:44.748 --> 00:03:50.894
bullets. Ernest had nails, a saw, and a hammer while other tools stuck out of his

49
00:03:50.895 --> 00:03:57.008
pockets. "Look at these nice sharp hooks!" said little Franz, proudly showing us a box of

50
00:03:57.009 --> 00:04:02.247
fishhooks. "Well done, Franz!" I said. "Those may save our lives by keeping us

51
00:04:02.248 --> 00:04:05.490
fed." "I have some good news," said my dear

52
00:04:05.491 --> 00:04:13.350
wife. "Some useful animals are still alive: a cow, a donkey, two goats, six sheep, and a fine

53
00:04:13.351 --> 00:04:16.955
pig." "Now we just have to figure out how to get ashore," I

54
00:04:16.956 --> 00:04:21.457
said. "Can't we each get into a big tub and float there?" said

55
00:04:21.458 --> 00:04:24.527
Jack. "I've often sailed like that around the pond at

56
00:04:24.528 --> 00:04:26.888
home." "A splendid idea!" I

57
00:04:26.889 --> 00:04:32.834
said. We soon found four tall wooden casks floating in the

58
00:04:32.835 --> 00:04:39.609
hold. After dragging them on deck, we managed—with some difficulty—to saw them in half.

59
00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:44.478
I was proud of our work, but my wife looked in dismay at the eight tubs.

60
00:04:44.479 --> 00:04:47.959
"I'll never get into one of those!" she declared.

61
00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:50.067
"Just wait," I said.

62
00:04:50.068 --> 00:04:55.394
I arranged my tubs in a row, connecting them with planks along the bottom and sides.

63
00:04:55.395 --> 00:05:01.783
When I finished, I was confident our boat would be perfectly safe in smooth water.

64
00:05:01.784 --> 00:05:10.666
There was only one problem: Our boat was so heavy that we couldn't move it an inch.

